<section xml:id="sec-ch03-03-ChainRule" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Multi-Variable Chain Rule</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      In this section we will consider two different cases when taking
      derivatives:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            You have a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> where <m>x=x(t)</m> and
            <m>y=y(t)</m> (both <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are themselves
            functions of single variable <m>t</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            You have a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> where <m>x=x(s, t)</m> and
            <m>y=y(s, t)</m> (both <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are themselves
            functions of multiple variables <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Chain Rule in Case 1</title>

    <p>
      Given a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> where <m>x=x(t)</m> and
      <m>y=y(t)</m>, then the derivative of <m>f(x, y)</m> with respect to
      <m>t</m> is given by
      <md>
        \frac{d f}{d t}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{d x}{d t}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{d y}{d t}
      </md>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      In this case we can use the usual derivative notation since
      <m>f(x, y)</m> is a function of <alert>just the variable</alert>
      <m>t</m> if we replace <m>x</m> with <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y</m> with
      <m>y(t)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-03-chain-rule-case-1">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 4.5</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_09_10_3643f349c031f748fd41g-1.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>A dependency diagram showing z as a function of x and y, each depending on t, illustrating the chain rule case 1.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>A dependency diagram showing <m>z</m> as a function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, each depending on <m>t</m>, illustrating the chain rule case 1.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-03-ex-9-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find <m>z^{\prime}(t)</m>, where
        <m>z=x^{3}-y, x=(t-1)^{2}, y=t^{3}+1</m>, in the following ways:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) First make the substitutions <m>x=x(t)</m> and <m>y=y(t)</m>,
        and write <m>z</m> as a function of <m>t</m>.
      </p>

      <p>(b) Using the Chain Rule.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Substituting <m>x=(t-1)^2</m> and <m>y=t^3+1</m> into <m>z</m>
        writes <m>z</m> as a function of just the variable <m>t</m>:
        <md>
          z(t) = \left( (t-1)^2 \right)^3 - (t^3+1) = (t-1)^6 - t^3 - 1
        </md>
        Differentiating with respect to <m>t</m>, using the single-variable
        chain rule on <m>(t-1)^6</m>:
        <md>
          z'(t) = 6(t-1)^5 - 3t^2
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The partial derivatives of <m>z</m> and the derivatives of <m>x</m>
        and <m>y</m> are
        <md>
          \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = 3x^2, \quad \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} = -1, \quad \frac{dx}{dt} = 2(t-1), \quad \frac{dy}{dt} = 3t^2
        </md>
        so by the Chain Rule
        <md>
          \frac{dz}{dt} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \frac{dx}{dt} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} \frac{dy}{dt} = 3x^2 \cdot 2(t-1) + (-1) \cdot 3t^2
        </md>
        Substituting <m>x=(t-1)^2</m> to express everything in terms of
        <m>t</m>:
        <md>
          z'(t) = 3\left( (t-1)^2 \right)^2 \cdot 2(t-1) - 3t^2 = 6(t-1)^5 - 3t^2
        </md>
        which agrees with the answer from part (a).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The Chain Rule is an important tool because in many applications, you
      don't know specific expressions for <m>f(x, y), x(t)</m> or
      <m>y(t)</m>, but do know the corresponding rates of change.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-03-ex-9-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the Cobb-Douglas Production function <m>P(L,K)</m> where
        <m>P</m> represents production, <m>K</m> represents availability of
        capital, and <m>L</m> represents availability of labor. Suppose that
        both <m>L</m> and <m>K</m> are also functions of time <m>t</m>
        (which makes sense as the availability of capital and labor may
        change depending on time). Moreover, assume that
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>L</m> increases by 2 units when <m>t</m> increases 1 unit
              and <m>P</m> increases by 7 units when <m>L</m> increases 1
              unit.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>K</m> increases by 0.5 units when <m>t</m> increases 1 unit
              and <m>P</m> increases by 3 units when <m>K</m> increases 1
              unit.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        When <m>t</m> increases by 1 unit, how many units does <m>P</m>
        increase by?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Here <m>P</m> depends on the intermediate variables <m>L</m> and
        <m>K</m>, and both are functions of <m>t</m>, so by the Chain Rule
        <md>
          \frac{dP}{dt} = \frac{\partial P}{\partial L} \frac{dL}{dt} + \frac{\partial P}{\partial K} \frac{dK}{dt}
        </md>
        The given information is exactly these four rates of change:
        <m>\frac{dL}{dt} = 2</m> and <m>\frac{\partial P}{\partial L} = 7</m>
        from the first bullet point, and <m>\frac{dK}{dt} = 0.5</m> and
        <m>\frac{\partial P}{\partial K} = 3</m> from the second. Hence
        <md>
          \frac{dP}{dt} = 7(2) + 3(0.5) = 14 + 1.5 = 15.5
        </md>
        so when <m>t</m> increases by 1 unit, production <m>P</m> increases
        by approximately 15.5 units. Notice that we never needed explicit
        formulas for <m>P(L,K)</m>, <m>L(t)</m>, or <m>K(t)</m>; the rates
        of change were enough.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      The Chain Rule formula works no matter how many <q>intermediate
      variables</q> you have. For example, suppose <m>w=f(x, y, z)</m> and
      <m>x=x(t), y=y(t)</m>, and <m>z=z(t)</m> (here, <m>x, y, z</m> are
      your intermediate variables). Then
      <md>
        \frac{d f}{d t}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{d x}{d t}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{d y}{d t}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial z} \frac{d z}{d t}
      </md>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Chain Rule in Case 2</title>

    <p>
      Given a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> where <m>x=x(s, t)</m> and
      <m>y=y(s, t)</m>, then the partial derivatives of <m>f</m> with
      respect to <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> have the following formulas:
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp \frac{\partial f}{\partial s}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial s}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial s}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      Here we must use the partial derivative notation since even if we
      substitute <m>x=x(s, t)</m> and <m>y=y(s, t), f</m> is still a
      function of two variables ( <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> ).
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-03-chain-rule-case-2">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 4.5</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_09_10_3643f349c031f748fd41g-4.png" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>A dependency diagram showing z as a function of x and y, each depending on u and v, illustrating the chain rule case 2.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>A dependency diagram showing <m>z</m> as a function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, each depending on <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>, illustrating the chain rule case 2.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-03-ex-9-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>z=cos(x) \sin (y^2)</m> where <m>x=s+t</m> and <m>y=s-t</m>.
        Compute <m>z_{t}</m> in the following ways:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) First make the substitutions <m>x=x(s, t)</m> and
        <m>y=y(s, t)</m>, and write <m>z</m> as a function of <m>s</m> and
        <m>t</m>.
      </p>

      <p>(b) Using the Chain Rule.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Substituting <m>x=s+t</m> and <m>y=s-t</m> into <m>z</m>:
        <md>
          z(s,t) = \cos(s+t) \sin\left( (s-t)^2 \right)
        </md>
        Differentiating with respect to <m>t</m> (treating <m>s</m> as a
        constant), using the product rule and the single-variable chain
        rule:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>z_t = -\sin(s+t) \sin\left( (s-t)^2 \right) + \cos(s+t) \cos\left( (s-t)^2 \right) \cdot 2(s-t)(-1)</mrow>
          <mrow>= -\sin(s+t) \sin\left( (s-t)^2 \right) - 2(s-t) \cos(s+t) \cos\left( (s-t)^2 \right)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The partial derivatives of <m>z</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>y</m> are
        <md>
          \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = -\sin(x) \sin(y^2), \quad \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} = 2y \cos(x) \cos(y^2), \quad \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} = 1, \quad \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} = -1
        </md>
        so by the Chain Rule
        <md>
          z_t = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} = -\sin(x) \sin(y^2)(1) + 2y \cos(x) \cos(y^2)(-1)
        </md>
        Substituting <m>x=s+t</m> and <m>y=s-t</m>:
        <md>
          z_t = -\sin(s+t) \sin\left( (s-t)^2 \right) - 2(s-t) \cos(s+t) \cos\left( (s-t)^2 \right)
        </md>
        which agrees with the answer from part (a).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      This formula works no matter how many <q>intermediate variables</q>
      you have. For example, suppose <m>w=f(x, y, z)</m> and
      <m>x=x(s, t), y=y(s, t)</m>, and <m>z=z(s, t)</m> (here,
      <m>x, y, z</m> are your intermediate variables). Then
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp \frac{\partial w}{\partial s}=\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial s}+\frac{\partial w}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial s}+\frac{\partial w}{\partial z} \frac{\partial z}{\partial s}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp \frac{\partial w}{\partial t}=\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial w}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial w}{\partial z} \frac{\partial z}{\partial t}</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Restricting the Domain of a Surface to a Curve</title>

    <p>
      Given a surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, we sometimes are only interested in
      certain parts of the surface. Specifically, we can restrict the inputs
      of a surface to a curve in its domain by using appropriate
      substitutions <m>x=x(t)</m> and <m>y=y(t)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-03-ex-9-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we are walking on the surface of a part of a sphere
        <m>z=\sqrt{4-(x-1)^{2}-(y-2)^{2}}</m> but we only want to walk above
        the line <m>y= x+1</m> in its domain. We can do this by substituting
        <m>x=t</m> and <m>y= t+1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-03-ex-9-4">
        <caption>The sphere along with curve on the sphere above the line parametrised by x = t, y = t plus 1</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_10_3643f349c031f748fd41g-6. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-03-ex-9-4" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_10_3643f349c031f748fd41g-6.png">
              <shortdescription>The sphere along with curve on the sphere above the line parametrised by x = t, y = t plus 1</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The sphere along with curve on the sphere above the line parametrised by <m>x = t</m>, <m>y = t+1</m></p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <m>y= x+1</m> in purple and <m>z(t, t+1)</m> in yellow
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Making the substitutions <m>x=t</m> and <m>y=t+1</m> restricts the
        inputs of the surface to the line <m>y=x+1</m>, and the height of
        the surface becomes a function of the single variable <m>t</m>:
        <md>
          z(t) = \sqrt{4 - (t-1)^2 - \left( (t+1)-2 \right)^2} = \sqrt{4 - (t-1)^2 - (t-1)^2} = \sqrt{4 - 2(t-1)^2}
        </md>
        This is the yellow curve in the picture. Note that for <m>z(t)</m>
        to be defined we need
        <m>4 - 2(t-1)^2 \geq 0 \Rightarrow (t-1)^2 \leq 2 \Rightarrow 1-\sqrt{2} \leq t \leq 1+\sqrt{2}</m>;
        these are exactly the values of <m>t</m> for which the line lies
        underneath the part of the sphere.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-03-ex-9-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the surface of a part of a sphere
        <m>z=\sqrt{4-(x-1)^{2}-(y-2)^{2}}</m>  and the curve <m>C</m> in the
        <m>x y</m>-plane given parametrically as <m>x= \cos (t)+0.5</m> and
        <m>y= \sin (t)+1.5</m>, where <m>0 \leq t \leq 2 \pi</m>.
      </p>

      <p>(a) Find <m>z^{\prime}(t)</m>.</p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-03-ex-9-5">
        <caption>The sphere along with curve on the sphere above the circle parametrised by x = cosine of t plus 0.5, y = sine of t plus 1.5</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_10_3643f349c031f748fd41g-7. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-03-ex-9-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_10_3643f349c031f748fd41g-7.png">
              <shortdescription>The sphere along with curve on the sphere above the circle parametrised by x = cosine of t plus 0.5, y = sine of t plus 1.5</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The sphere along with curve on the sphere above the circle parametrised by <m>x = \cos(t)+0.5</m>, <m>y = \sin(t)+1.5</m></p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <m>(x-0.5)^2+(y-1.5)^2=1</m> in purple and
        <m>z( \cos (t)+0.5,  \sin (t)+1.5)</m> in yellow. blue
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) Imagine that you are walking on the surface directly above the
        curve <m>C</m> (the surface restricted to inputs coming from
        <m>C</m> ). Find the values of <m>t</m> for which you are walking
        uphill ( <m>z(t)</m> is increasing).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We use the Chain Rule. Treating <m>y</m> and <m>x</m> as constants
        respectively, the partial derivatives of <m>z</m> are
        <md>
          \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = \frac{-(x-1)}{\sqrt{4-(x-1)^2-(y-2)^2}}, \quad \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} = \frac{-(y-2)}{\sqrt{4-(x-1)^2-(y-2)^2}}
        </md>
        and the derivatives of the parametrization are
        <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = -\sin(t)</m> and <m>\frac{dy}{dt} = \cos(t)</m>.
        Hence
        <md>
          z'(t) = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \frac{dx}{dt} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} \frac{dy}{dt} = \frac{(x-1)\sin(t) - (y-2)\cos(t)}{\sqrt{4-(x-1)^2-(y-2)^2}}
        </md>
        Substituting <m>x-1 = \cos(t)-0.5</m> and <m>y-2 = \sin(t)-0.5</m>,
        the numerator becomes
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\left( \cos(t)-0.5 \right)\sin(t) - \left( \sin(t)-0.5 \right)\cos(t)</mrow>
          <mrow>= \cos(t)\sin(t) - 0.5\sin(t) - \sin(t)\cos(t) + 0.5\cos(t) = 0.5\left( \cos(t) - \sin(t) \right)</mrow>
        </md>
        and the expression under the square root becomes
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>4 - \left( \cos(t)-0.5 \right)^2 - \left( \sin(t)-0.5 \right)^2 = 4 - \left( \cos^2(t) + \sin^2(t) - \cos(t) - \sin(t) + 0.5 \right)</mrow>
          <mrow>= 2.5 + \cos(t) + \sin(t)</mrow>
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          z'(t) = \frac{0.5\left( \cos(t) - \sin(t) \right)}{\sqrt{2.5 + \cos(t) + \sin(t)}}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We are walking uphill when <m>z'(t) \gt 0</m>. Since
        <m>\cos(t)+\sin(t) \geq -2</m>, the denominator satisfies
        <m>\sqrt{2.5+\cos(t)+\sin(t)} \geq \sqrt{2.5-2} \gt 0</m>, so the
        sign of <m>z'(t)</m> is the sign of the numerator
        <m>0.5\left( \cos(t)-\sin(t) \right)</m>. Another argument that
        arrives in the same conclusion is that denominator is always
        non-negative (as a square root) and well defined (not zero). Solving
        <m>\cos(t) = \sin(t)</m> on <m>[0, 2\pi]</m> gives
        <m>\tan(t) = 1 \Rightarrow t = \frac{\pi}{4}</m> or
        <m>t = \frac{5\pi}{4}</m>. Testing a value in each of the resulting
        intervals: at <m>t=0 \in [0,\frac{\pi}{4}]</m> we get
        <m>\cos(0)-\sin(0) = 1 \gt 0</m>; at
        <m>t=\pi\in [\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{5\pi}{4}]</m> we get
        <m>\cos(\pi)-\sin(\pi) = -1 \lt 0</m>; and at
        <m>t=\frac{3\pi}{2}\in [\frac{5\pi}{4},2\pi]</m> we get
        <m>\cos\left(\frac{3\pi}{2}\right)-\sin\left(\frac{3\pi}{2}\right) = 0-(-1) = 1 \gt 0</m>.
        Hence <m>z(t)</m> is increasing, and we are walking uphill, for
        <md>
          0 \leq t \lt \frac{\pi}{4} \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{5\pi}{4} \lt t \leq 2\pi
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
